#23 positive.go:207:2-14 - go-deprecated-ioutil: prefer the io and os functions instead of the deprecated io/ioutil package, which only wraps them since Go 1.16 (`iu.WriteFile` -> `os.WriteFile`).
#24 positive.go:208:9-20 - go-deprecated-ioutil: prefer the io and os functions instead of the deprecated io/ioutil package, which only wraps them since Go 1.16 (`iu.ReadFile` -> `os.ReadFile`).
#25 positive.go:211:17-28 - go-interface-any: prefer using `any` instead of `interface{}`, as it's shorter and easier to read (`interface{}` -> `any`).
#26 positive.go:216:13-34 - go-time-since: prefer using `time.Since` or `time.Until` instead of subtracting `time.Now()`, as they state the intent directly (`time.Now().Sub(start)` -> `time.Since(start)`).
#27 positive.go:217:10-34 - go-time-since: prefer using `time.Since` or `time.Until` instead of subtracting `time.Now()`, as they state the intent directly (`deadline.Sub(time.Now())` -> `time.Until(deadline)`).
#28 positive.go:219:3-66 - go-errors-new-sprintf: prefer using `fmt.Errorf` instead of `errors.New(fmt.Sprintf(...))`, as it formats the message in one call (`errors.New(fmt.Sprintf("failed %d times after %s", n, elapsed))` -> `fmt.Errorf("failed %d times after %s", n, elapsed)`).
#29 positive.go:220:3-37 - generic-error-message: error raised with a generic message that names neither what failed nor the value (`errors.New("Something went wrong")`).
#30 positive.go:220:14-36 - go-error-string-format: error strings should start lowercase and end without punctuation, as callers usually print them inside other messages (`"Something went wrong"` -> `"something went wrong"`).
#31 positive.go:221:14-31 - go-error-string-format: error strings should start lowercase and end without punctuation, as callers usually print them inside other messages (`"deadline in %s."` -> `"deadline in %s"`).
#32 positive.go:222:14-27 - go-error-string-format: error strings should start lowercase and end without punctuation, as callers usually print them inside other messages (`"disk full\n"` -> `"disk full"`).
#33 positive.go:227:5-32 - go-strings-contains: prefer using `strings.Contains` instead of comparing the result of `strings.Index`, as it says what is tested (`strings.Index(s, sub) != -1` -> `strings.Contains(s, sub)`).
#34 positive.go:227:36-65 - go-strings-contains: prefer using `strings.Contains` instead of comparing the result of `strings.Index`, as it says what is tested (`strings.IndexAny(s, "xy") < 0` -> `!strings.ContainsAny(s, "xy")`).
#35 positive.go:228:10-34 - go-bytes-equal: prefer using `bytes.Equal` instead of `bytes.Compare` for equality checks, as it is faster and easier to read (`bytes.Compare(a, b) == 0` -> `bytes.Equal(a, b)`).
#36 positive.go:230:9-37 - go-strings-contains: prefer using `strings.Contains` instead of comparing the result of `strings.Index`, as it says what is tested (`bytes.IndexRune(a, 'x') >= 0` -> `bytes.ContainsRune(a, 'x')`).
#37 positive.go:230:41-83 - go-strings-equal-fold: prefer using `strings.EqualFold` instead of comparing case-converted strings, as it compares without allocating 2 new strings (`strings.ToLower(s) != strings.ToLower(sub)` -> `!strings.EqualFold(s, sub)`).
#38 positive.go:239:3-18 - go-defer-in-loop: deferred calls in a loop should be avoided, as they run when the function returns instead of at the end of each iteration (`defer f.Close()` runs when the function returns).
#39 positive.go:241:2-33 - go-range-over-int: prefer using `for i := range n` instead of a three-clause loop counting from zero, as it's more concise and easier to read (`for i := 0; i < len(paths); i++` -> `for i := range len(paths)`).
#40 positive.go:244:2-24 - go-range-over-int: prefer using `for i := range n` instead of a three-clause loop counting from zero, as it's more concise and easier to read (`for i := 0; i < 3; i++` -> `for range 3`).
#41 positive.go:247:2-19 - go-redundant-blank-range: prefer omitting blank identifiers at the end of a range clause, as they add nothing (`for k, _ := range` -> `for k := range`).
#42 positive.go:250:2-15 - go-redundant-blank-range: prefer omitting blank identifiers at the end of a range clause, as they add nothing (`for _ = range` -> `for range`).
